Strict alignment for the riscv64 JIT (JitRiscV64StrictAlign, off by default).

SP1 rejects every misaligned load/store and OpenVM's load/store chip only
proves the aligned shifts, but RyuJIT only knows about the accesses the front
end flags unaligned (fixup perf-35 expands those byte-wise). Everything else
it assumes aligned, which holds for object fields, array elements, stack
locals and statics but not for byrefs into spans, Unsafe.As reinterpretation
or pointer arithmetic: one mainnet block on SP1 trips ~68k misaligned
accesses in JIT'd Nethermind code (Hash256 ctor, EVM push/mload, Keccak, RLP,
Trie) and in the BCL's software Vector128 paths.

With the knob on, Lowering proves alignment structurally (object ref + const
offset, scaled index with scale a multiple of the width, LEA over those,
LCL_ADDR offsets, handles) and marks every other scalar IND/STOREIND and every
unrolled block copy/init with an unknown source or destination as unaligned.
Codegen then emits an inline alignment test with the single wide instruction
as the fast path and the perf-35 byte-wise sequence as the slow path, so the
common aligned case costs andi+bnez. Block copies test both addresses at once
(or/andi/bnez) and fall back to the byte loop. Stack locals at an offset
that is not a multiple of the width (GT_LCL_FLD/GT_STORE_LCL_FLD, e.g. a
stackalloc buffer reinterpreted at an odd offset, and dependently promoted
fields of packed structs reached through GT_LCL_VAR) are accessed byte-wise.
bflat passes the knob for --libc sp1 and openvm. Applies on top of perf-35.

